# Rendering PHP Arrays as HTML Tables

Converting a multidimensional array into an HTML table is a frequent requirement when building data-heavy views in PHP applications. You often have raw data stored in variables and need to transform that structure into semantic markup for the browser. Consider a scenario where you are displaying flower inventory:

```php
$shop = array(
    array("rose",   1.25, 15),
    array("daisy",  0.75, 25),
    array("orchid", 1.15, 7 ),
);
```

Your goal is to render this as a table with headers for `title`, `price`, and `number`. While modern frameworks handle this abstraction automatically, understanding the underlying mechanics helps when building custom components or working within legacy codebases.

## The Basic Loop Approach

The most straightforward method involves iterating over the rows of your array and generating `<tr>` elements. You can use a nested loop to construct the table structure. The outer loop iterates through each row (sub-array), while the inner loop extracts individual cells.

```php
echo "<table border='1'>";
echo "<thead><tr>";
echo "<th>title</th><th>price</th><th>number</th></tr></thead>";
echo "<tbody>";

foreach ($shop as $row) {
    echo "<tr>";
    foreach ($row as $cell) {
        echo "<td>" . htmlspecialchars($cell) . "</td>";
    }
    echo "</tr>";
}

echo "</tbody></table>";
```

This approach works reliably across different PHP versions. However, it relies on array indices matching the header order exactly. If your data changes or if you need to add a new column later without updating every loop iteration, this method becomes brittle.

## Using `array_walk` for Cleaner Logic

For larger datasets or more complex rendering logic, `array_walk` can provide a cleaner separation between data and presentation. You can pass an array of headers and a callback function that processes each row. This keeps the main view code concise:

```php
$headers = ['title', 'price', 'number'];

echo "<table>";
echo "<thead><tr>";
foreach ($headers as $h) {
    echo "<th>$h</th>";
}
echo "</tr></thead>";

echo "<tbody>";
array_walk($shop, function($row, $index) use ($headers) {
    echo "<tr>";
    foreach ($headers as $colIndex => $colName) {
        $value = isset($row[$colIndex]) ? $row[$colIndex] : '';
        echo "<td>$value</td>";
    }
    echo "</tr>";
});
echo "</tbody></table>";
```

This pattern decouples the column names from the raw data structure. It ensures that even if the `$shop` array contains fewer or more columns than expected, you can control exactly how missing values are handled within the callback.

## Security and Data Sanitization

A critical pitfall in dynamic table generation is injecting unsanitized data directly into HTML attributes or content. In the examples above, `htmlspecialchars()` is essential to prevent Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. If a flower name contains `<script>` tags, they must be escaped before rendering.

Additionally, consider how you handle numeric types. The `price` column might come as a float, while `number` could be an integer or string. Ensure consistent formatting if your output needs to match specific locale requirements (e.g., using `number_format()` for currency).

## Framework Integration: Laravel

If you are working within the Laravel ecosystem, you likely prefer not to echo raw HTML strings directly from PHP logic. Instead, you should leverage Blade templates. Passing the `$shop` array to a view allows you to separate concerns effectively.

```php
// In your controller
return view('shop.index', ['shop' => $shop]);
```

In your Blade file (`resources/views/shop/index.blade.php`), you can use the `@foreach` directive:

```blade
<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>title</th>
            <th>price</th>
            <th>number</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        @foreach($shop as $item)
            <tr>
                <td>{{ $item[0] }}</td>
                <td>{{ $item[1] }}</td>
                <td>{{ $item[2] }}</td>
            </tr>
        @endforeach
    </tbody>
</table>
```

Laravel's Blade engine automatically escapes output variables by default, reducing the risk of XSS without needing explicit `htmlspecialchars` calls in every instance. For more advanced layouts, you might explore packages that simplify complex data grids, such as those available on <a href="https://laravelcompany.com">LaravelCompany</a>, which can help abstract table rendering logic further.

## Performance Considerations

For small to medium-sized lists, the performance difference between a `foreach` loop and other methods is negligible. However, if you are generating tables with thousands of rows, avoid heavy string concatenation inside loops. Building the table HTML in a single string buffer or using an array to collect rows before joining them can reduce memory overhead and improve rendering speed in high-traffic environments.
